 to task outcomes or the restart protocol: account creation, limits file write, and the reboot sequence. The reboot leaves /tmp/reboot.log and new systemd-private ephemeral dirs, which are expected, low-risk byproducts of a native reboot rather than executor-introduced residue; no failed services, package inconsistency, or unsafe exposure appear in the after_executor snapshot versus after_prepare. No unnecessary or unexplained mutations were found in the timeline.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-b1af08a6510a4616\",\"cmd-ed805e57d4124d42\",\"global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files\",\"global:after_executor:node1:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node1:package-health\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"Both material outcomes of the task — creating the appworker account and constraining its login sessions to 8,192 open files and 256 processes via pam_limits — are directly satisfied with pre- and post-restart verification. The independently required node-restart evidence protocol is also fully and coherently demonstrated: healthy pre-restart baseline, acknowledged reboot, a differing post-restart boot identity corroborated by the fixed lifecycle snapshots, and confirmed recovery of the account and its limits. No unnecessary mutations or hygiene concerns were identified.\",\"limitations\":[\"The pam_limits enforcement was verified via 'sudo -u appworker -i' rather than an actual interactive SSH login session for appworker, which is a reasonable and commonly accepted proxy but not a fully independent login-session test.\",\"The after_prepare snapshot is an alias of before_prepare (prepare was disabled), so the preparation_comparison shows no changes by construction; this does not affect the executor-caused change analysis, which correctly uses after_prepare vs after_executor.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"account-created","requirement":"Create the appworker account on node1","status":"satisfied","summary":"Confirmed appworker did not exist beforehand (cmd-7